%I A087280 #50 Aug 21 2026 08:34:39
%S A087280 5,8,10,12,24
%N A087280 Solutions k of max(m+d(m)) = k+2 for m<k; d(m) is the number of divisors of m.
%C A087280 It is not known if there are other terms. There are no more terms < 10^10.
%C A087280 There are no more terms <= 10^12. - _Patrik Idén_, Jun 13 2026
%D A087280 R. K. Guy, Unsolved Problems in Number Theory, section B8.
%H A087280 Paul Erdős, <a href="https://www.jstor.org/stable/2689842">Some Unconventional Problems in Number Theory</a>, Math. Mag. 52(2) (Mar. 1979), 67-70. See p. 68 (Problem 4).
%H A087280 Patrik Idén, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.21134668">Computational Verification of Erdős Problem 647 up to 10^12: Gap-Growth Analysis, Depth-Record Extension to D=11, and Structural Analysis</a>, Zenodo preprint v3, 2026.
%H A087280 Ibrahim Mian and Shayaan Siddique, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.17880">A Kernel-Checked Exclusion Certificate for Erdős Problem 647</a>, arXiv:2608.17880 [cs.LO], 2026. See pp. 1, 3, 6.
%e A087280 The largest value of m+d(m) for m < n=8 is 6+d(6)=10 = n+2, so 8 is in the sequence.
%o A087280 (PARI) isok(k) = if (k>1, vecmax(vector(k-1, m, m+numdiv(m))) == k+2); \\ _Michel Marcus_, Aug 21 2026
%Y A087280 Cf. A062249.
%K A087280 nonn,more,changed
%O A087280 1,1
%A A087280 _Jud McCranie_, Aug 28 2003
